== An Appearance of Love

VI. It is no evidence that religious affections are saving, or that they are otherwise, that there is an appearance of love in them.

There are no professing Christians who claim that this is an argument against the truth and saving nature of religious affections. But on the other hand, there are some who suppose it is good evidence that affections are from the sanctifying and saving influences of the Holy Spirit. Their argument is that #emph[Satan cannot love,] this affection being directly contrary to the devil, whose very nature is enmity and malice. And it is true that nothing is more excellent, heavenly and divine than a spirit of true Christian love to God and men. It is more excellent than #emph[knowledge,] or #emph[prophecy,] or #emph[miracles,] or #emph[speaking in the tongues of men and of angels.] It is the chief of the graces of God’s Spirit, and the life, essence and sum of all true religion. It is that by which we are most conformed to heaven, and most contrary to hell and the devil. But even so, it is bad reasoning to argue from this that there are no counterfeits of it. It may be observed that the more excellent anything is, the more counterfeits of it there will be. So there are many more counterfeits of silver and gold than of iron and copper. There are many false diamonds and rubies, but who sets out to counterfeit common stones? The more excellent things are, the more difficult it is to make anything that will be like them in their essential nature and inward virtue. Yet the more numerous the counterfeits will be, and the more art and subtlety will be exercised and displayed in an exact imitation of the outward appearance. So there is the greatest danger of being cheated in buying the medicines that are most excellent and powerful, though it is most difficult to imitate them with anything of the same value and virtue, and their counterfeits are good for nothing when we have them. So it is with Christian virtues and graces. The subtlety of #emph[Satan] and men’s deceitful hearts are usually exercised chiefly in counterfeiting those that are in the highest repute. So there are perhaps no graces that have more counterfeits than love and humility, these being the virtues in which the beauty of a true Christian especially appears.

But as to love, it is plain from the Scripture that persons may have a kind of religious love and yet have no saving grace. Christ speaks of many professing Christians who have such love, whose love will not continue, and so they will fall short of salvation: “Because of the multiplication of wickedness, the love of many will grow cold. But the one who endures to the end will be saved” (Matt 24:12–13). These last words plainly show that those spoken of before, whose love would not #emph[endure to the end] but would #emph[grow cold,] would not be saved.

Persons may seem to have love to God and Christ, indeed to have very strong and violent affections of this kind, and yet have no grace. For this was evidently the case with many graceless #emph[Jews,] such as those who praised Jesus so highly, following him day and night without food, drink or sleep, such as the one who said, “I will follow You wherever You go,” and those who cried, “Hosanna to the Son of David!”#footnote[In agreement with this Mr. #emph[Stoddard] observes, in his #emph[Guide to Christ,] that some sinners #emph[have pangs of affection, and give an account that they find a spirit of love to God, and that they aim at the glory of God, having that which closely resembles saving grace,] and that #emph[sometimes their common affections are stronger than saving ones.] And he supposes that at times natural men may have such #emph[violent pangs of false affection to God that they may think themselves willing to be damned.] Page 21, and 65.]

The apostle seems to suggest that there were many in his day who had a counterfeit love to Christ, in Ephesians 6:24: “Grace to all who love our Lord Jesus Christ #emph[in sincerity.]” The last word in the original means #emph[in incorruption.] This shows that the apostle had a sense that there were many who had a kind of love to Christ whose love was not pure and spiritual.

So also Christian love to the people of God may be counterfeited. It is evident from the Scripture that there may be strong affections of this kind without saving grace, as there were in the #emph[Galatians] toward the apostle #emph[Paul,] when they were ready to tear out their eyes and give them to him. And yet the apostle expresses his fear that their affections had come to nothing, and that he had spent his labor on them in vain (Gal 4:11, 15).
